WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
llama.

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.721
cpp can often be used through prebuilt binaries or built from source at a high level.

00:00:06.721 --> 00:00:09.707
Prebuilt binaries are convenient for getting started quickly.

00:00:09.707 --> 00:00:14.186
Building from source can provide more control over acceleration options, platform support,

00:00:14.186 --> 00:00:15.305
and compiler settings.

00:00:15.305 --> 00:00:19.037
The right path depends on operating system, hardware, comfort level,

00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:23.889
and whether the system is a short experiment or a repeatable AI workstation.

00:00:23.889 --> 00:00:27.248
Windows, Linux, and macOS each have their own considerations.

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:30.234
CPU-only builds are usually the simplest starting point.

00:00:30.234 --> 00:00:34.712
GPU-enabled builds may use acceleration paths such as CUDA, Metal, Vulkan, ROCm,

00:00:34.712 --> 00:00:38.071
or other backend options depending on platform and hardware.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:42.549
Those paths can improve performance, but they also introduce driver, compiler, library,

00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:44.042
and version compatibility concerns.

00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:46.282
Installation should be documented and repeatable.

00:00:46.282 --> 00:00:47.401
Record the llama.

00:00:47.401 --> 00:00:51.880
cpp version or release, operating system, driver stack, build options, acceleration backend,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.612
compiler or dependency notes, model file, and working command examples.

00:00:55.612 --> 00:01:00.090
This is especially important when a setup supports real workflows or when

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.076
you expect to reproduce it on another machine.

00:01:03.076 --> 00:01:06.062
Avoid random version mixing without a rollback plan.

00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:11.287
A runtime update, driver update, model change, or new build option can improve performance,

00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:14.645
but it can also break a previously stable workflow.

00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:18.004
Stable local inference comes from controlled changes, known-good configurations,

00:01:18.004 --> 00:01:20.340
and basic validation after each meaningful update.
